      "type": "llm_response",
      "reasoning": "I’ll cast Cathar’s Companion. I have enough mana and it’s the most efficient play. For {2}{W}, I get a 3/1 with lifelink that can immediately boost another creature. With Emiel the Blessed on the battlefield, whenever a creature enters, I may pay {G/W} to put a +1/+1 counter on it — two counters if it’s a Unicorn. But Cathar’s Companion isn’t a Unicorn, so it’ll only get one counter if I pay.\n\
\n\
But more importantly, Cathar’s Companion has: \"When this enters, choose a target creature you control. That creature gets +1/+1 and gains vigilance until end of turn.\" I’ll target Emiel the Blessed — making it 5/5 with vigilance. That turns Emiel into a massive, hard-to-block threat.\n\
\n\
Casting it now sets up lethal damage next turn. Emiel alone could attack for 5 fliers (if it loses summoning sickness), and with my other creatures, I’m looking at 10+ damage soon.\n\
\n\
Tap two Plains and a Thriving Heath for {2}{W}, cast Cathar’s Companion targeting Emiel the Blessed.\n\
\n\
Then, if the ability triggers, I’ll pay {G/W} to give Emiel a +1/+1 counter, making it 6/6 with vigilance. That’s a huge, resilient threat.\n\
\n\
Cast now.\n\
\n\
\n\
\n\
\n\
\n\
\n\
\n\
\n\
